TOOL_RESULT retrieve_history:
{"results": [], "n_results": 0}

Stage 2. Using the retrieval result above (follow-up tools are allowed within the tool budget), complete steps 4 to 9
and reply with the final action. Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_concepts": [
  "concept"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "question"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a record returned by a tool",
   "status_as_recorded": "outcome status exactly as stated in the record (supported | rejected | inconclusive | untestable | artifact | not a hypothesis test | no result recorded)",
   "finding": "what the record states, in its own terms"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a returned record",
   "what_failed": "approach",
   "why": "failure mechanism or limitation as recorded"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "what the retrieved evidence establishes for this question"
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "what it leaves open or cannot show"
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "your inference, not stated in any record"
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "uncertainty or missing information"
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes | no | no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "whether and how the historical evidence changed the decision"
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{"action": "final", "output": {
  "research_concepts": [
    "player absence",
    "offensive reorganization",
    "points per possession change",
    "structural influence L",
    "predictive modeling",
    "displacement D_real"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "How to operationally define reorganization relative to L (e.g., D_real/L_pre, or interaction of D_real and L_pre)?",
    "What inclusion criteria for absence spells ensure reliable baseline and exposure windows?",
    "How to separate the reorganization effect from confounders like opponent quality and concurrent injuries?",
    "How to address regression to the mean in change scores of points per possession?",
    "Is the sample of 148 spells sufficient to detect a plausible effect after controlling for covariates?"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [],
  "failed_approaches": [],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "No historical evidence was available for this specific question."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "No prior findings constrain the expected direction, magnitude, or measurability of the effect."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "Reorganization relative to L is likely operationalized as the ratio of structural displacement D_real to pre-absence structural influence L_pre (or as D_real while controlling for L_pre moderating).",
    "Absence spells of at least 15 consecutive games with adequate surrounding games can be extracted from the available per-game data (2022-23 through 2025-26).",
    "Change in points per possession is inherently noisy; controlling for pre-absence PPP and opponent strength is essential to reduce confounding and regression to the mean.",
    "The sample size of 148 spells appears modest but could support a moderate effect if the signal-to-noise ratio is not too low; however, power is a primary concern.",
    "The play-type classifier and structural influence L have known measurement error, which will attenuate the estimated relationship.",
    "No prior project work was retrieved, so the current assessment relies entirely on methodological reasoning."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "The exact distribution of L_pre and D_real in this sample; extreme values could cause leverage issues.",
    "The stability of play-type classification across seasons, especially with rule or roster changes (drift).",
    "Whether the 148 spell count includes both regular season and playoffs or just regular season; mixing competition stages might introduce heterogeneity.",
    "The best functional form of the predictor: ratio, interaction, or separate terms."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "no_historical_evidence",
    "explanation": "No prior records were returned by the retrieval tool, so the decision is based solely on the current assessment of feasibility, data, and risks without influence from earlier work."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.65,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
        "text": "Structural influence L and displacement D_real are derived from play-type classifications that have documented inferrer error; measurement noise will attenuate the relationship."
      },
      {
        "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
        "text": "With 148 spells, power to detect a small-to-medium effect while controlling for multiple confounders is borderline; a narrow null result would be ambiguous."
      },
      {
        "code": "CONFOUNDING",
        "text": "Opponent defensive quality, other simultaneous player absences, and schedule difficulty during the absence window can confound the change in points per possession independently of reorganization."
      },
      {
        "code": "RTM_SELECTION",
        "text": "If spells are selected on extreme pre-absence PPP, the change score will regress to the mean regardless of reorganization."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "unknown",
    "cited": [],
    "summary": "No retrieved earlier project work addressed this exact predictive question."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "The 'reorganization relative to L' metric is not a standard validated measure; its construction (e.g., D_real/L_pre) depends on inferrer accuracy for play-type labels and on the structural influence algorithm, both of which carry measurement error."
    },
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "The effective sample size is 148 spells; with likely small effect sizes and several control variables, the analysis risks being underpowered, increasing the chance of a false negative or inflated effect estimates."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Change in PPP is influenced by opponent defensive quality, schedule (home/away, rest days), concurrent injuries to other teammates, and coaching adjustments that are not captured by the reorganization metric. Failure to fully adjust for these could produce spurious associations."
    },
    {
      "code": "RTM_SELECTION",
      "text": "Absence spells may be preceded by periods of particularly strong or weak offensive performance; using raw change scores invites regression to the mean, which can be misinterpreted as a reorganization effect."
    },
    {
      "code": "LABEL_ERROR",
      "text": "Play-type misclassification (especially for PNR vs. drive, or transition vs. spot-up) directly affects both the structural fingerprint and the derived L and D measures, potentially biasing the predictor."
    },
    {
      "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
      "text": "For players with a narrow set of play types or very high L, the possible range of D_real may be restricted, limiting variability in the predictor."
    },
    {
      "code": "LEAKAGE_LOOKAHEAD",
      "text": "Care must be taken that L_pre is computed only from games before the absence and that the fingerprint is not updated with any information from the absence window, or else the predictor will be contaminated by outcome information."
    },
    {
      "code": "DRIFT_NONSTATIONARITY",
      "text": "Offensive systems and league-wide play-type distributions may shift over the four-season span; pooling across seasons without accounting for drift could introduce heterogeneity."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.25,
    "rejected": 0.20,
    "untestable": 0.30,
    "artifact": 0.10,
    "uncertain": 0.15
  },
  "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The slope of the change in team points per possession (during-absence minus pre-absence) regressed on the relative reorganization measure (recommended: D_real / L_pre, or an interaction term between D_real and L_pre), while conditioning on pre-absence PPP, average opponent defensive rating during the absence, number of other key player absences, and a linear time trend.",
    "unit": "Absence spell: a stretch of at least 15 consecutive missed regular-season games by a single player, anchored by a pre-absence window of at least 10 games and a during-absence window of at least 10 games, all in the same season.",
    "sample": "All available regular-season games from the 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25, and 2025-26 seasons; players who miss 15+ consecutive games and meet the window requirements. Spells that span the regular-season/playoff boundary are excluded. Approximately 148 spells are anticipated.",
    "power": "With 148 observations and an assumed true incremental R² of 0.06–0.10 (medium effect) for the reorganization predictor after covariates, power for a two-sided t-test on the coefficient at α=0.05 is about 0.55–0.75. We will supplement frequentist tests with a Bayesian regression reporting the posterior probability of a positive effect.",
    "controls": "Pre-absence team PPP (to adjust for base level and RTM), mean opponent defensive rating during absence games, count of other rotation players concurrently absent, proportion of home games in the absence window, spell length in games, and a linear season trend (day of season).",
    "falsification": "1) Permutation test: randomly reassign the reorganization values across spells to create a null distribution of the coefficient. 2) Placebo absence: apply the same analysis to windows where no player is absent but the same number of games is sampled from the same team-season, expecting no effect. 3) Negative control: test whether reorganization predicts change in opponent PPP, where no direct mechanistic link should exist.",
    "multiplicity": "One primary predictor is specified (the relative reorganization measure). No subgroup analyses or alternative definitions will be tested without Holm-Bonferroni correction. Any exploratory alternative metrics will be explicitly labeled as secondary.",
    "decision_rule": "Primary inference is based on the 95% credible interval of the reorganization coefficient from a Bayesian linear model with weakly informative priors (normal(0,1) for standardized coefficients). The hypothesis is supported if the interval excludes zero and is consistent with the predicted direction (higher relative reorganization → smaller PPP decline). A sensitivity frequentist p < 0.05 is also reported. If the interval includes zero but is narrow around zero, we declare the effect practically null only if the region of practical equivalence (ROPE) argument is satisfied.",
    "data_version": "NBA play-by-play and box-score data up to 2026-11-01, processed with the project's play-type classifier (inferrer v2.3) and structural influence code (commit hash or tag to be pinned at pre-registration). Only regular-season data used; garbage time excluded (definition: final 4 minutes of fourth quarter with lead > 15 points)."
  }
}}

